course: dictionary
lesson: 14595
title: Plumage, a bird's feathers
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Plumage means all the feathers of a bird, including their colours and patterns. Think of it as a bird's coat. «The peacock's plumage shone blue and green in the sun.» The clues are peacock and shone blue and green: the word must be about the feathers, because feathers are what shine in the sun.
explain: Plumage is an uncountable noun, so there is no plural. You say some plumage, never a plumage or two plumages. «The mallard had brown plumage, which helped her hide on the nest.» Brown is a clue that the feathers help a bird blend in.
explain: Words in science lessons help you think about why animals look the way they do. Male birds often have brighter plumage to attract females. Females are often duller, so they are harder to spot when they sit on the nest. Birds moult, which means they shed old feathers and grow new plumage.
fact: Plumage means all the feathers of a bird, with their colours and patterns.
fact: «Plumage» is uncountable: say some plumage, not a plumage.
fact: «The peacock's plumage shone blue and green in the sun.»
fact: Male birds often have brighter plumage to attract females, while females are often duller to hide on the nest.
fact: Birds moult, which means they shed old feathers and grow new plumage.
exercise: «The peacock's plumage shone blue and green in the sun.» What does plumage mean? | *All the feathers of a bird, with their colours and patterns | The nest a bird builds in spring | The sound a bird makes when it sings | A kind of tall tree
exercise: Why might male birds often have brighter plumage than females? | To help them swim faster in rivers | To make them sleepy | *To attract females | To hide from cats in the long grass
exercise: «The mallard's plumage was dull and brown, so she could hide on the nest.» Find and copy the word that means a bird's feathers. | nest | hide | *plumage | mallard
test: Which word best describes the feathers of a bird, including their colours and patterns? | Nest | *Plumage | Juvenile | Moult
test: Which phrase is correct? | two plumages | three plumages | a plumage | *some plumage
exam: Why does a bird moult? | *To shed old feathers and grow new plumage | To lay its eggs in the water | To learn to sing new songs | To change its name
